# 3. Scale Up

## Application Server vs Web Server

| | Web Server | Application Server |
|---|---|---|
| Primary role | Handles HTTP/HTTPS protocol, serves static files | Executes business logic, generates dynamic content |
| Typical software | Nginx, Apache | Gunicorn, PHP-FPM, Puma, uWSGI, Node.js |
| What it serves | HTML, CSS, JS, images (pre-built files on disk) | Database-driven pages, API responses, session logic |
| Communicates with | Client browsers (and proxies to app server) | Web server (upstream) and database (downstream) |
| Stateless? | Yes — just reads files | Often stateful (sessions, caches, business state) |
| CPU/memory profile | Low — mostly I/O bound | Higher — compute-bound for complex logic |

In a minimal setup both roles run on one machine. Separating them lets each tier be scaled, secured, and tuned independently.

